      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE AND METHODS",
          "content": "We examined the prognostic significance of electrocardiographic change during anginal attack, C-reactive protein and fibrinogen in 169 patients with unstable angina."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "During the 90-day follow-up period, 26 patients (15%) exhibited new cardiac events (death, myocardial infarction or urgent revascularization). Using multivariate analysis, ST depression (relative risk 7.507 [95 % confidence intervals 1.842-30.592], p<0.01) during anginal attack was found to be an independent risk factor to predict cardiac events as well as diabetes mellitus, an increased total cholesterol level and the use of a thrombolytic agent. C-reactive protein and fibrinogen did not have prognostic significance."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "ST depression during anginal attack is an independent risk predictor for new cardiac events in patients with unstable angina."
        }
      ]},
